<TopicPage slug="subjects/languages/intermediate/grammar-without-tears">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Grammar has a reputation problem, and it is a sequencing problem. Taught first, as
    rules to memorise before speaking, grammar produces people who can conjugate verbs
    and cannot hold a conversation. Avoided entirely, it produces fluent plateau-riders
    whose errors never quite leave. The method that works integrates both in a specific
    order: <strong>notice the pattern in real input, then study it consciously, then
    use it in output</strong> — patterns before paradigms, function before form. This
    lesson is that sequence, with the tactics that make it run.
  </p>
  <p>
    The A2 stage of the CEFR ladder frames the destination: "communicate in simple and
    routine tasks", "describe your background and immediate environment" — communication
    is the goal and grammar is its scaffolding. The scaffolding still has to hold: what
    changes is when you install it and in what form.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="descriptive patterns, chunks, the notice–study–use loop, error data" widget="equationStep" config={gramCfg} />

  <h2>Descriptive versus prescriptive: learn the real rules</h2>
  <p>
    First, separate the two meanings of "grammar". <strong>Descriptive grammar</strong> is
    what speakers of the language actually do — the system the linguistics lessons
    formalised. <strong>Prescriptive grammar</strong> is etiquette: style-guide rules like
    "no split infinitives" (Star Trek's "to boldly go" violates it and is perfect
    English), "never end a sentence with a preposition", and their equivalents in every
    language — usually eighteenth- and nineteenth-century inventions, often borrowed from
    Latin, and frequently ignored by excellent writers. Learners waste years on the
    etiquette of a language they cannot yet order coffee in. Learn the descriptive
    system first; acquire the register rules later, when you can feel which situations
    they govern. (Every language does have real register rules — politeness levels,
    formal endings — and those are descriptive facts about the language, not etiquette
    about yours.)
  </p>

  <h2>Chunks: the brain's preferred grammar format</h2>
  <p>
    Corpus research keeps finding that fluent speech is largely <strong>formulaic</strong>
    — assembled from prefabricated multi-word units rather than built word-by-word from
    rules (major studies put well over half of running discourse in chunks). Your first
    language does this ("as it turns out", "at the end of the day", "would you mind…"),
    and the second language must too. So learn grammar's most load-bearing patterns as
    wholes: "I would have…", "if I were…", "there is/are", "I have to…", "used to",
    "getting + comparative". A chunk learned whole is immediately fluent; its internal
    grammar can be dissected later, when the analysis has somewhere to land. This is the
    "patterns before paradigms" principle: the paradigm (full conjugation table) is the
    reference document, the chunks are the acquisition vehicle — and chasing tables
    before chunks is the single most common self-taught-learner mistake.
  </p>

  <h2>The notice–study–use loop</h2>
  <p>
    The sequence this lesson exists to install. <strong>Notice</strong>: in your input
    hours, a pattern starts recurring — past tenses in a podcast, the conditional in a
    series. Noticing is a skill; Richard Schmidt's hypothesis (1990) made it central:
    what you do not consciously notice, you acquire slowly or never. <strong>Study</strong>:
    now open the reference — five minutes on what the pattern is called and how it
    works, with your collected examples in hand. Study sticks now because the rule
    explains something you have already seen ten times. <strong>Use</strong>: force
    yourself to produce the pattern deliberately for a week — in self-talk, in tutoring
    sessions, in writing. Serial focus beats parallel confusion: one pattern per cycle,
    to felt mastery, then the next. Run the loop for the twenty or thirty structures
    that carry your target language's load and you will have covered what a grammar
    textbook spreads over two years — in the order that actually installs.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked cycle. Suppose Spanish past tenses keep appearing. Notice: preterite for
    completed events, imperfect for backgrounds and habits — start tagging examples in
    your listening ("comí" versus "comía"). Study: ten minutes on the distinction, with
    your own examples as the exercises. Use: a week of narrating your day aloud, past
    tense only, with an error log. The rule was never the entry point; it was the
    sharpening stone.
  </p>

  <h2>Errors are data, not sins</h2>
  <p>
    The second-language learner's developing system has a name in the research —
    <strong>interlanguage</strong> — and its errors are systematic: they map exactly what
    your current internal grammar believes, often showing the same staged errors native
    children make (which is evidence of rule-building, not failure). Practical
    consequence: triage. <strong>Transient errors</strong> (the ones that change month
    to month) need nothing but input and time. <strong>Fossilised errors</strong> (the
    same mistake for a year) need targeted attention: record yourself, keep an error
    log, and run notice–study–use cycles aimed at the specific structure. The log is
    the diagnostic instrument — without it you are guessing at your own system, and the
    guesses are systematically wrong (you will fix what you already know how to fix,
    which is precisely what does not need fixing).
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> mistakes become permanent if "allowed". The evidence
  says otherwise: most errors are stages, and correction research shows the best use of
  feedback is focused and delayed — a note at the end of a conversation, one structure at
  a time — while constant interruption teaches avoidance, not accuracy. Errors are the
  curriculum; correction is a scheduling decision.</p>

  <h2>Case, gender and conjugation without tables</h2>
  <p>
    The feared topics — gender systems, case endings, verb conjugations — yield to the
    same machinery. <strong>Learn them through high-frequency chunks rather than
    tables</strong>: German der/die/das arrives in "der Mann", "die Frau", "das Kind"
    (bundles, as the 1,000-words lesson prescribed), then in high-frequency phrases
    ("ich habe einen…", "mit dem Auto"), and the case system assembles itself in your
    memory from hundreds of witnessed instances — with the table consulted afterward as
    a map, not swallowed beforehand as cargo. Finnish's fifteen cases become fifteen
    recurring phrase-patterns; Spanish gender becomes the article bundled to every noun
    at first meeting. The table is reference, the chunks are acquisition — the same
    division as the linguistics lesson's competence/performance split, applied to study
    habits.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked month: one pattern per week</h2>
  <p>
    The loop at programme scale. Week 1 — past narration: notice (tag every past-tense
    verb you hear in your daily input; aim for thirty), study (one session on the
    past system's shape — one past or two? regular vs irregular?), use (narrate each
    day aloud: what you did, five sentences minimum), log errors. Week 2 — future and
    intention ("going to", "will", the target language's own machinery): same cycle.
    Week 3 — description and comparison (adjectives, "more than / as … as", the
    structures that power the CEFR's "describe your environment"): notice in
    reviews, vlogs and recipes; use in daily descriptions of objects and places.
    Week 4 — the first conditional ("if … then"): the highest-value complex pattern
    for routine tasks. Each week: one pattern, four jobs, one log. A month of this
    covers what four months of textbook chapters cover, because every hour of input
    you are already doing becomes the exercise set — the loop reuses hours you were
    spending anyway.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading a reference grammar without drowning</h2>
  <p>
    The reference grammar and the table are tools, and like tools they need handling
    instructions. Use them as <strong>maps, not cargo</strong>: read the chapter's first
    page (the overview of what the system is for), skim the examples, and skip the
    exhaustive lists until a specific failure sends you looking. The metalanguage pays
    here: knowing the difference between tense and aspect (time location versus time
    shape — "I ate" versus "I was eating") tells you which paragraph of the reference
    answers which felt confusion; knowing what case is for makes a Finnish table
    readable instead of terrifying. The trick that makes reference reading stick:
    always arrive with a specimen — bring three real sentences from your input that
    confused you, and read the grammar until they stop being confusing. A grammar read
    answer-first is retained; the same grammar read cover-to-cover is not.
  </p>

  <h2>Sentence mining: the curriculum that assembles itself</h2>
  <p>
    The loop's fuel is <strong>mined sentences</strong> — sentences harvested from your
    own input that contain exactly one thing you do not own yet. The protocol: while
    listening or reading, capture anything that makes you pause (a chunk, a conjugation,
    a word order surprise); clip or copy it with its context; make it the card (the
    grammar lesson's chunk logic, automated by encounters); and re-read the capture
    pile weekly — the recurring structures in your own confusion are your personal
    grammar syllabus, ranked by how often they actually stop you. Mining beats
    textbook ordering for one decisive reason: frequency is measured against your
    life and your media, not against a syllabus written for an average student in
    1985. Ten mined sentences a day, reviewed by spaced repetition, compound into a
    grammar that is simultaneously accurate and yours.
  </p>

  <h2>The translation trap, and the free-translation exercise</h2>
  <p>
    Word-by-word translation from your first language is the default failure mode of
    self-taught grammar, and it has a signature: sentences that are grammatically
    assembled and idiomatically wrong — the direct-translation error that no rule table
    prevents. The counter-exercise is <strong>free translation</strong>: take two
    sentences of natural target-language audio (with transcript), translate them into
    your language, wait a day, then translate your own translation back — and compare
    with the original. The diffs are your interlanguage made visible: the structures
    you think you own but route around, the chunks you paraphrase instead of produce.
    Run it weekly on five sentences. It is uncomfortable (translation back is always
    harder than understanding) and disproportionately diagnostic — the exercise
    surfaces exactly the gap between recognising grammar and owning it, which is the
    gap this whole lesson exists to close.
  </p>

  <h2>What the grammar lessons above buy you</h2>
  <p>
    This site's linguistics strand is not separate from this one — it is the map of it.
    Knowing that the plural -s is a rule and not a list (the wug test) tells you why
    "two blorks" is learnable and why children say "foots" on the way to "feet". Knowing
    that word order carries the grammatical load in English (the morphology lesson's
    trade) tells you why word-order errors matter more in English than case endings do,
    and why German feels backwards only until you map its cases. Knowing that chunks
    carry over half of fluent speech tells you why the grammar lesson's method is
    chunk-first. And knowing what tense, aspect, agreement and case mean lets you read
    any language's reference grammar in hours. The metalanguage is reusable across
    every language you will ever learn — the rare study that never needs repeating.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The theory underneath, briefly, because it explains why the sequence works.
    <strong>Skill acquisition theory</strong> (DeKeyser and others) models grammar as
    moving declarative → procedural → automatised: rules you can state become behaviours
    through practice become reflexes through volume — and the stages need different
    inputs, which is why one method (rules only, or immersion only) undertrains part of
    the pipeline. <strong>U-shaped development</strong>: learners who sounded right,
    then wrong ("goed" after correctly saying "went"), then right again, are not
    regressing — the memorised forms are being reorganised by rules, exactly as in
    first-language acquisition. <strong>The interface debate</strong>: how much explicit
    knowledge converts into implicit competence remains contested, but the working
    consensus ("weak interface") matches this lesson's loop — explicit study helps you
    notice, noticing feeds acquisition, and output forces the procedural stage. The
    metalanguage pays across languages too: once you know what case, aspect and
    agreement are, every future language's grammar reads in hours instead of weeks —
    the rare investment that transfers everywhere.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Schmidt, "The Role of Consciousness in Second Language Learning", Applied Linguistics (1990); DeKeyser, Practice in a Second Language (2007); the linguistics ladder's syntax and morphology lessons; Batstone, Grammar (1994).</p>
